          "detail": "Cano currently lists Cascina Adelaide Langhe Nebbiolo, Ellena Giuseppe Barolo and Salvano Barbaresco. All three are built around Nebbiolo, so the useful skill is explaining why they are not interchangeable. Langhe Nebbiolo is the approachable entry point: fresh red fruit, savoury character and polished tannin; Cascina Adelaide specifically describes its version as an expression of Nebbiolo’s elegance and freshness rather than a “baby Barolo.” Barolo must be 100% Nebbiolo and ages at least three years; the official consortium describes it as powerful and elegant, with red fruit, rose/violet, spice, liquorice, cocoa, tobacco and leather, plus complex tannin. Barbaresco is also 100% Nebbiolo but requires at least two years’ ageing and is typically framed around aromatic elegance: raspberry/red fruit, violet, cinnamon/nutmeg, hay, wood, hazelnut and anise. Cano’s Salvano Barbaresco has been reviewed with tart/dried cherry, raspberry, rose, spice and firm but elegant tannin. Service shorthand: Langhe Nebbiolo for someone who wants Nebbiolo character without the weight/price of Barolo; Barbaresco for refinement; Barolo for the guest asking for the most structured, age-worthy Piedmont red. Pair all three naturally with beef, mushrooms, truffle-rich dishes and aged cheese; Barolo is the most obvious steak/Bistecca di Manzo recommendation.",
