          "detail": "Cano currently lists Cantina di Negrar Valpolicella Ripasso Superiore and an Amarone della Valpolicella Classico. Ripasso literally means “re-pass”: basic Valpolicella is fermented again on the pomace left from Amarone and/or Recioto, adding body, alcohol, phenolic material and richness. The Valpolicella consortium describes Ripasso as medium-bodied, dry, soft and velvety with red fruit, cherry, spice and vanilla; Cantina di Negrar’s version uses Corvina, Corvinone and Rondinella and is designed for grilled/braised red meat. Amarone is a different level of concentration: grapes are dried for roughly 100–120 days before fermentation, concentrating sugar and polyphenols; the finished wine is dry, high in alcohol, deeply structured and capable of long ageing. Expect dried/cherry fruit, spice and a full, warming texture. Service shorthand: Ripasso is the bridge between everyday Valpolicella and Amarone; recommend it when a guest wants richness without Amarone’s intensity or price. Amarone is for braised/grilled meat, mature cheese or someone explicitly asking for a powerful contemplative red.",
