          "detail": "Cano currently lists Casalbosco Chianti Superiore, Tenuta Aquilaia Super Tuscan and Antonio Gaudioso Brunello di Montalcino “Antonutluca.” For interview/service purposes, know the conceptual differences. Chianti Superiore is the familiar Tuscan Sangiovese lane: bright red-cherry fruit, acidity and savoury/herbal structure make it food-friendly for tomato sauces, pizza, pasta and medium-weight meat dishes. “Super Tuscan” is not a legal appellation; WSET describes it as an informal category for ambitious Tuscan reds that historically fell outside traditional Chianti rules—often Sangiovese blended with Cabernet/Merlot, Bordeaux-variety blends, or high-end pure Sangiovese, frequently labelled Toscana IGT. These can show darker fruit, more oak and more Cabernet-like structure than classic Chianti. Brunello di Montalcino is legally much stricter: 100% Sangiovese from Montalcino, at least two years in oak, and not released until the fifth year after harvest. The Brunello consortium describes it as dry, warm, lightly tannic, robust and harmonious, pairing naturally with red meat, game, mushrooms/truffles and mature cheese. Service shorthand: Chianti = bright/food-flexible; Super Tuscan = modern, often fuller/darker; Brunello = prestigious, structured Sangiovese built for steak or a special bottle.",
